proofs.proof_tex:
Fix a cohort \(s\), an admissible query \(z_0\), and a labelled point
\(\xi\in\mathcal M_{N,T,\mathbf n}(z_0)\).  All probabilities below are under \(\mathbb K_\xi\).  Since \(0<\lambda\le1\), the order convention gives \(r=0\), so \(G_{sT}(z_{0s},b)\) is scalar.  Continuity and local positivity of \(K\) give constants \(k_0>0\) and \(\eta\in(0,\delta_0]\) such that \(K(v)\ge k_0\) whenever \(\|v\|_2\le\eta\).  The ball \(B(z_{0s},\eta b)\) is inside the cube for \(b\le1\).  Writing \(v_d\) for the Lebesgue volume of the unit Euclidean ball, the density lower bound in Definition \texttt{def:fixed-query-kernel-class} therefore gives, uniformly in \(t\),
\[
 p_{tb}:=\Pr\{\|X_{st}-z_{0s}\|_2\le\eta b\}
 \ge c_Xv_d\eta^db^d=:c_pb^d.                                 \tag{1}
\]
If \(M_b\) counts these visits, then
\[
 G_{sT}(z_{0s},b)\ge\frac{k_0M_b}{Tb^d}.                        \tag{2}
\]

Set
\[
 a_T=\left\lceil\frac{3\log(T\vee2)}{|\log\rho_0|}\right\rceil.
\]
Partition the calendar into consecutive blocks of length \(a_T\) and split the complete blocks by parity.  Within either parity, consecutive retained blocks are separated by a complete block.  By the definition of the beta coefficient, the joint law of two sigma-fields at that separation differs in total variation from the product of their marginals by at most \(\beta_0\rho_0^{a_T}\).  Inductively insert one product marginal at a time.  The triangle inequality and contraction of total variation under marginalization show that the joint law of all retained blocks differs from the product of its block marginals by at most the sum of the successive beta coefficients.  For both parities together this total variation error is at most
\[
 2\left\lceil\frac{T}{a_T}\right\rceil\beta_0\rho_0^{a_T}
 \le CT^{-2}.                                                    \tag{3}
\]
For every design-block event, the difference between its probability under the original and product laws is at most (3), by the variational definition of total variation.  This comparison concerns only the design blocks and makes no assertion about response-block independence.

For one independent comparison block let \(Y_j\in[0,a_T]\) be its number of visits.  Convexity places \(e^{-y/a_T}\) below the chord joining its values at zero and \(a_T\), so (1) gives
\[
 \mathbb E e^{-Y_j/a_T}
 \le1-\frac{1-e^{-1}}{a_T}\mathbb EY_j
 \le\exp(-cb^d).                                                \tag{4}
\]
Apply exponential Markov to the independent comparison blocks in either parity and then add the coupling error (3).  There are constants \(c_1,C_1>0\) such that
\[
 \Pr\{M_b\le c_1Tb^d\}
 \le C_1\exp(-c_1Tb^d/a_T)+C_1T^{-2}                           \tag{5}
\]
whenever \(Tb^d\ge C_1a_T\); enlarging \(C_1\) makes the bound trivial below that scale.  Compatibility of \(c_G\) with the population local-constant Gram bound makes (2) at least \(c_G\) off the event in (5).

Put
\[
 b_\circ=(Tn_s)^{-1/(2\lambda+d)}\vee N^{-1/2}
          \vee(a_T/T)^{1/d}.                                    \tag{6}
\]
The smallest dyadic candidate not below a number in \([b_{0s},1]\) is at most twice that number.  Apply (5) at candidate scales of order \(2^jb_\circ\).  Apply the single total-variation comparison (3) to the union of all bad-scale events before taking a union bound under the product law; hence its \(CT^{-2}\) error is charged only once.  The independent-block exponential tails then give
\[
 \Pr\{h_s^\star>C2^jb_\circ\}
 \le C\exp(-c2^{jd})+CT^{-2},\qquad j\ge0,                     \tag{7}
\]
until the scale reaches one; afterward the probability is zero.  Dyadic tail summation, charging the common coupling-failure event only once, yields
\[
 \mathbb E(h_s^\star)^{2\lambda}
 \le Cb_\circ^{2\lambda}+CT^{-2}.                              \tag{8}
\]
If \(b_\circ\) is bounded away from zero, (8) follows directly from \(h_s^\star\le1\).  Since
\(a_T\le C\log(T\vee2)\) and \(2\lambda/d\le2\), (6)--(8) imply
\[
 \mathbb E(h_s^\star)^{2\lambda}
 \le C\left\{(Tn_s)^{-2\lambda/(2\lambda+d)}+N^{-\lambda}
 +\left(\frac{\log(T\vee2)}{T}\right)^{2\lambda/d}\right\}. \tag{9}
\]
Cauchy--Schwarz gives the asserted bound for
\(\mathbb E(h_s^\star)^\lambda\).

At \(b=1\), equations (1)--(5) imply
\(\Pr\{G_{sT}(z_{0s},1)<c_G\}\le CT^{-2}\).  If this event fails for no cohort, \((z_{0s},1)\) belongs to every fold--cohort anchor set, so fallback is impossible.  A union bound over fixed \(S\) gives
\[
 \sup_{\xi\in\mathcal M_{N,T,\mathbf n}(z_0)}
 \Pr_{\mathbb K_\xi}(\mathsf F_{\mathrm{fb}})\le CT^{-2}.     \tag{10}
\]
All constants are uniform because the fixed-query density and mixing constants are uniform.  In the independent-calendar subclass take \(a_T=1\) and omit (3); this replaces the last term in (9) by \(T^{-2\lambda/d}\).  For a panel law, the defining query-conditional assumptions put its labelled regular conditional point in this fixed-query class for marginal-almost-every admissible query, so the result transfers exactly on that conull set.
